      <!-- Section 11: How Capital Losses Work -->
      <section class="guide-block">
        <h2 class="guide-title">How Capital Losses and Tax-Loss Harvesting Work</h2>
        <p>
          Capital losses are the silver lining of an unprofitable investment. When you sell a capital asset for less than its adjusted purchase price and allowable acquisition costs, you incur a <strong>capital loss</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here is how capital loss relief operates across international jurisdictions (note that because Singapore imposes 0% capital gains tax, capital losses cannot be claimed as tax deductions in Singapore):
        </p>
        <ul class="guide-bullet-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Dollar-for-Dollar Offset:</strong> In the US, UK, Australia, India, and Canada, realized capital losses can offset realized capital gains dollar-for-dollar in the same tax year.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Indefinite Carryforward:</strong> If your capital losses exceed your capital gains in a given tax year, the net unused loss can be carried forward indefinitely into future tax years to shield future investment profits.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>US Ordinary Income Offset ($3,000 Rule):</strong> In the United States, if net capital losses exceed all capital gains, individual taxpayers can deduct up to <strong>$3,000 per year</strong> ($1,500 if married filing separately) directly against ordinary salary or business income.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Australia Pre-Discount Ordering:</strong> Under ATO tax law, you must deduct your accumulated capital losses from your gross capital gain <em>before</em> applying the 50% CGT discount. You cannot apply the discount first and then deduct the loss.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Wash Sale &amp; Bed-and-Breakfasting Rules:</strong> In the US, the <strong>30-day Wash Sale Rule</strong> prevents taxpayers from claiming a tax loss if they buy substantially identical securities within 30 days before or after the sale. In the UK, the <strong>30-day Bed &amp; Breakfasting Rule</strong> enforces a similar restriction. In Canada, the <strong>Superficial Loss Rule</strong> denies loss deductions if you repurchase the same asset within 30 calendar days.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

