      <!-- Section 12: Frequently Asked Questions -->
      <section class="guide-block" aria-labelledby="cgt-faqs-heading">
        <h2 id="cgt-faqs-heading" class="guide-title">Frequently Asked Questions About Capital Gains Tax</h2>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What is a capital gains tax calculator?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              A capital gains tax calculator is an online estimation tool that computes the tax liability owed when you sell an investment, property, or valuable personal asset for more than your purchase price and allowable acquisition and selling expenses.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How do I calculate capital gains tax?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              First, subtract your cost basis (purchase price plus allowable buying and selling fees) from your sale proceeds to establish your gross gain. Next, deduct any statutory allowances, exemptions, or prior-year capital losses. For Australia and Canada, apply the legal 50% discount or statutory inclusion rate. Finally, apply the applicable statutory tax brackets for your country.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Is capital gains tax the same in every country?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              No. Tax structures vary dramatically: the US uses preferential long-term brackets (0%, 15%, 20%); the UK uses unified flat rates (18% and 24%) above an exemption; Australia adds 50% of discounted gains to assessable income; India applies special rates (12.5% LTCG, 20% STCG) plus 4% cess; and Canada applies a two-tier inclusion rate (50% up to $250k; 66.67% above) taxed at progressive ordinary rates.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What are the US capital gains tax rates for 2026?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Federal long-term capital gains rates for 2026 are 0%, 15%, or 20% depending on filing status and ordinary income. Short-term gains (held 1 year or less) are taxed as ordinary income from 10% to 37%. High earners are also subject to a 3.8% Net Investment Income Tax (NIIT).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What is the UK Capital Gains Tax allowance for 2026/27?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              For 2026/27, the UK individual Annual Exempt Amount is £3,000. Chargeable gains above this threshold are taxed at 18% for basic rate taxpayers and 24% for higher and additional rate taxpayers across both equities and residential property.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does the Australian CGT discount work?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Individual Australian resident taxpayers who hold an asset for 12 months or longer receive a 50% CGT discount. After subtracting allowable capital losses from the gross gain, the remaining profit is halved, and only that remaining 50% is added to taxable income and taxed at your marginal rate plus the 2% Medicare levy.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How is capital gains tax calculated in India for FY 2026–27?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Under the simplified Budget 2024 framework, listed equity shares and equity mutual funds held over 12 months are taxed at 12.5% on profits exceeding the ₹1.25 lakh annual exemption. Short-term equity gains are taxed at 20%. Real estate held over 24 months is taxed at 12.5% without indexation. All taxes include a 4% Health and Education cess.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does Canada's capital gains inclusion rate work in 2026?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              In Canada, individuals face a 50% inclusion rate on their first $250,000 of net capital gains in a calendar year, and a 66.67% (two-thirds) inclusion rate on gains exceeding $250,000. This taxable gain is added to your income and taxed at combined federal and provincial brackets.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Do I pay capital gains tax on stocks?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Yes, selling stocks, ETFs, or mutual funds held in taxable brokerage accounts triggers capital gains tax if sold for a profit. However, securities held inside qualified tax-sheltered accounts—such as a US Roth IRA/401(k), UK Stocks &amp; Shares ISA, Canadian TFSA, or Australian superannuation fund—are completely exempt from capital gains tax upon sale.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Do I pay capital gains tax when selling my primary home?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Most nations offer substantial primary residence relief: UK Private Residence Relief (PRR) and Canadian Principal Residence Exemption (PRE) provide 100% tax exemption; the Australian Main Residence Exemption provides full exclusion; and US Section 121 allows individuals to exclude up to $250,000 (single) or $500,000 (married filing jointly) in profit.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Can capital losses reduce my capital gains tax?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Yes. In all five supported countries, realized capital losses offset capital gains dollar-for-dollar. Unused net capital losses can generally be carried forward indefinitely to shield future capital gains from taxation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Is capital gains tax calculated on the sale price or the profit?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Capital gains tax is never calculated on the gross sale price. It is assessed solely on your net gain (profit), which is your sale proceeds minus your original purchase price and allowable acquisition, improvement, and selling expenses.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

